== Seeking Marriage

=== §. 1. #emph[Of those who may seek to be married.]

Having in the former treatise laid down the foundation of all domestical duties, by expounding the words of the Apostle, I purpose now distinctly to lay them forth in order: beginning with the first and chiefest couple in the family, #emph[man] and #emph[wife.]

Here we are first to speak of the #emph[persons] who are to be accounted true and lawful #emph[husband] and #emph[wife:] and then of the #emph[duties] which they owe each to other.

So as this Treatise consisteth of two parts.

- 1\. The first declareth, who are man and wife.
- 2\. The second noteth out those common mutual duties which they owe one to another. First of the first.

Husband and wife are they who are rightly joined together #footnote[Who are husband and wife.] by the bond of marriage. #footnote[See #emph[Treat.] 1. §. 82. #emph[&] 84.] Whereby #emph[two are made one flesh.]

For the better clearing of this point, we will consider both the #emph[parties] that may be so nearly joined together, and also the #emph[manner] how they are rightly joined in so firm an unity.

About the #emph[parties] we are to search,

- 1\. Who may seek a mate for marriage.
- 2\. What kind of mate is to be taken.

1. All they who are able without inevitable danger to their #footnote[Who may marry.] yoke fellow to perform the essential duties of marriage, may be married.

Out of this proposition arise three questions to be discussed.

- 1\. Who are to be accounted #emph[able?]
- 2\. What danger is inevitable?
- 3\. Whether marriage be free for all but such?

=== §. 2. #emph[Of ripeness of years in them that are to be married.]

1. They are to be accounted able who have #footnote[#emph[Ephebi. Vt iustas nuptias contrahant, sint masculi puberes, foeminae viripotentes. Iustin. Instit. l.] 1. #emph[tit.] 10.] past the flour of their age, and are not by defect of nature, or any other occasion made impotent.

1. Ripeness of years is absolutely necessary for consummating a just and lawful marriage: wherefore as God at first, made #emph[Adam] of full age, so when he sought out a wife for him, he made her of full age too: he made her a #emph[woman,] not a child. #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 22. 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 36.] Where the Apostle adviseth parents to take care for the marriage of their children, he putteth in this proviso, #emph[if they pass the flour of their age.] Child-hood is counted the flour of age. While the flour of the plant sprouteth, the seed is green, unfit to be sown.

#emph[Quest.] How long lasteth the flour of age?

#emph[Answ.] #footnote[#emph[Justin. Instit. l.] 1. #emph[tit.] 22. Second part of #emph[Reports of Cases.] 35. #emph[Hen.] 6. #emph[fol.] 41. #emph[b.]] The civil law, and common law also, set down twelve years for the flour of a females age, and fourteen, of a males; which is the least: for before those years they can have no need of marriage, nor yet are well fit for marriage, so as if they forbear some years longer, it will be much better for the parties themselves that marry, #footnote[#emph[Est iuvenum coniugium incommodum liberorum procreationi. Cum enim in omnibus animantibus imperfecti sunt iuniorum partus, ac multo etiam magis foeminarum, id etiam fiat in hominibus necesse est. Arist. Pol. lib.] 7.] for the children which they bring forth, for the family whereof they are the head, and for the common wealth whereof they are members. Note the ages of the Kings of Israel and Judah when they were first married, and we shall find few of them to be under twenty, and those few, not above one or two years under: and yet of all sorts of people the Kings did use to marry the soonest, that so they might have heirs betimes.

#emph[Object.] #footnote[1 #emph[Chr.] 22. 5. #emph[&] 29. 1.] #emph[Solomon] was but a child when he came to the crown, and yet #footnote[2 #emph[Chro.] 12. 13.] he had then a child of a year old at least.

#emph[Answ.] He was said to be a child not simply, but comparatively, #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 2. 22.] in relation to his other brothers which were elder then he, & #footnote[#emph[&] 3. 7.] in regard of that great work he was to undertake. In the time of his reign he is said to be #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 11. 4.] #emph[old:] which could not be if he had been in years a child when he began to reign: for he reigned but forty years.

2. #emph[Object. Ahaz] was but twenty year old when he began to reign, and reigned but sixteen years, and yet when he died #emph[Hezekiah] his son was five and twenty year old, #footnote[2 #emph[Chro.] 28. 1. #emph[&] 29. 1.] by which computation #emph[Ahaz] had a child when he was but eleven, or twelve year old at most.

1. #emph[Answ.] Some say that this was extraordinary, and render this reason, #emph[Ahaz so young a father as Elizabeth an old mother,] #footnote[#emph[Broughton] in his Concent of Script.] #emph[should have hoped in Emmanuel of a virgin.]

2. #emph[Answ.] The beginning of that reign when #emph[Ahaz] was but twenty year old, is to be referred to #emph[Jotham] his father; for #emph[Ahaz] was twenty year old when #emph[he] (namely #emph[Jotham)] began to reign: as #footnote[2 #emph[Chro.] 36. 9.] #emph[Jehoiachin] was eight year old, when #emph[he] (namely his father #emph[Jehoiachim)] began to reign: for #emph[Jehoiachin] was #footnote[2 #emph[King.] 24. 8.] eighteen, when he himself began to reign.

#emph[Object. Jotham] was but #footnote[2 #emph[Chro.] 27. 1.] twenty and five year old when he began to reign, how then could #emph[Ahaz] his son at that time be twenty?

#emph[Answ. Jotham] was five and twenty year old when his father #emph[Uzziah] was struck with leprosy, from which time he #footnote[2 #emph[Chro.] 26. 21.] reigned as King even in his fathers life time. But after his father was dead, the kingdom was established to him alone #footnote[Parents sin in marrying children under age.] after a more solemn manner; in which respect it is said that then he began to reign: and then was his son #emph[Ahaz] twenty year old, #emph[Jotham] himself being about forty.

Contrary to the forenamed fitness of age is the practise of #footnote[#emph[Puella dum ei aetas matura non est, nulli dari vel promitti debet. Aug. epist.] 233. #emph[Matrimonium contractum verum non est si impubes fuerit qui contraxit. Azpilcuet. Consil. & resp. l.] 4. #emph[de spons. Cons.] 8.] such parents, or other friends of children, as make matches for them in their child-hood, and move them to consent, and so cause them to be married: such marriages are mock-marriages, and mere nullities. For children cannot know what appertaineth to marriage, much less can they perform that which is required of married persons: their consent therefore is justly accounted no consent, unless they do ratify it after they come to years.

=== §. 3. #emph[Of impotent persons that ought not to seek after marriage.]

2. They are to be accounted #emph[impotent,] and in that respect unable to perform the essential duties of marriage, who (to use the Scripture phrase) #emph[were born Eunuchs from their mothers] #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 12.] #emph[womb:] or by any accidental occasion are so made: as they who are defective, or closed in their secret parts: or taken with an incurable palsy: or possessed with frigidity, or any other such like impediment.

These ought not to seek after marriage: for by those signs of impotency God showeth that he calleth them to live single.

Contrary to this manifestation of Gods will do they sin, #footnote[A sin to conceal any impotency.] who conceal their impotency and join themselves in marriage, whereby they frustrate one main end of marriage, which is procreation of children; and do that wrong to the party whom they marry, as sufficient satisfaction can never be made.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of barrenness, that it hindereth not marriage.]

#strong[#emph[Quest.] Are such as are barren to be ranked among those impotent persons?]

#emph[Answ.] No, there is great difference betwixt impotency and barrenness.

1. Impotency may by outward sensible signs be known #footnote[#emph[Sterilem coniugem fas non est relinquere. Aug. de Nup. lib.] 1. #emph[cap.] 10.] and discerned, barrenness cannot: it is not discerned but by want of child-bearing.

2. Impotent persons cannot yield due benevolence: but such as are barren may.

3. Impotency is incurable: but barrenness is not simply so. Many after they have been a long while barren have become fruitful: and that not only by an extraordinary work of God above the course of nature (as #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 18. 11.] #emph[Sarah] and #footnote[#emph[Luk.] 1. 7.] #emph[Elizabeth,] with whom by reason of age it ceased to be after the manner of women) but also by such a blessing as might stand with the course of nature, being obtained by prayer (as #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 25. 21.] #emph[Rebekah] and #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 1. 5, 20.] #emph[Hannah)] whereof daily experience giveth good evidence: for many after 10, 15, 20, and more years barrenness have brought forth children.

On these grounds many Saints, who have been barren, have married, and their practise therein not disallowed, nor their marriage dissolved. #footnote[#emph[Tantum valet sociale vinculum coniugum, vt cùm causa procreandi colligitur, nec ipsa causa procreandi soluetur. Aug. de bon. Coniug. cap.] 7.] For though procreation of children be one end of marriage, yet is it not the only end: and so inviolable is the marriage bond, that though it be made for childrens sake, yet for want of children it may not be broken.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of that inevitable danger which hindereth marriage.]

3. They who are infected with such contagious diseases, as diffuse themselves into those who have society with them, and infect them also, ought not to seek after marriage: for that cannot but turn to the danger of the party with whom they marry. It was for mutual good one of another #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 18.] that God ordained the law of marriage; to use it to the hurt and danger of one another, is against the main end of the first institution.

The law of shutting up a leper from all society with men proveth as much; for if lepers might not have mutual society #footnote[2 #emph[Chro.] 26. 21.] with any man, much less might they have matrimonial society with a wife or an husband.

By contagious diseases not only both the parties which company together will be infected, but also their issue: whereby their disease which otherwise might die with themselves, is propagated to their posterity. A like restraint may be applied to such foul and loathsome diseases, as make the company and society of that person, who is infected therewith, irksome, and odious to their companion.

Contrary to the end and use of marriage do they sin, #footnote[A sin to conceal a contagious disease.] who conceal such diseases, and so join themselves in marriage, to the unanswerable prejudice of the party whom they marry.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of the lawfulness of marriage to all sorts of persons.]

Where there is no such just impediment as hath been before mentioned, it is lawful for all sorts of people of what #footnote[ἐν πᾶσι.] calling or condition soever to marry. For #emph[marriage is honourable] #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 13. 4.] #emph[in all,] or among all, namely in, or among all sorts of people: whereupon it is accounted #emph[a Doctrine of devils to forbid] #footnote[1 #emph[Tim.] 4. 1, 3.] #emph[to marry.] For it is a Doctrine contrary to Gods word, and a Doctrine that causeth much inward burning, and outward pollution, and so maketh their bodies, which should be temples of the Holy Ghost, to be sties of the devils.

The disease, for the redressing whereof marriage is sanctified, is a common disease which hath infected all sorts of people: why then shall not the remedy be as common?

In this case the Apostle saith indefinitely of all, without exception of any, #emph[to avoid fornication let every man have his own] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 2, 9.] #emph[wife, and let every woman have her own husband.] And again, #emph[If they cannot contain let them marry: for it is better to marry then to burn.]

#emph[Object.] #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 12.] #emph[There be Eunuchs which have made themselves Eunuchs] (that is, have abstained from marriage and lived in a single #footnote[A devilish doctrine to forbid marriage.] life continently) for the kingdom of heavens sake.

#emph[Answ.] That is spoken of some particular persons to whom the gift of continency was given: not of any distinct conditions, and callings, as if all and every one of this or that calling had so done or were able so to do: whereupon Christ addeth this clause, #emph[He that is able to receive it, let him receive it,] and the Apostle to the same purpose saith, #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 7.] #emph[every one hath his proper gift of God.]

Contrary to this necessary and warrantable liberty, is the impure and tyrannical restraint of the Church of Rome, whereby all that enter into any of their holy orders, are kept from marriage.

Do they not herein #footnote[#emph[Act.] 15. 10.] #emph[tempt God by putting a yoke upon mens necks, which neither our fathers nor we are able to bear?] #footnote[#emph[Vide Chrysost. hom.] 4. #emph[in Isay] 6.] No such restraint was ever enjoined by Gods word to any of those holy functions which he ordained: for under the Law it was lawful for high Priests, ordinary Priests, all sorts of Levites, and extraordinary Prophets, to marry: and under the Gospel, for #footnote[#emph[Omnes Apostoli exceptis Iohanne & Paulo vxores habuerunt. Ambr. in] 2 #emph[Cor.] 11.] Apostles, Bishops, Deacons, and all Ministers of the word. #footnote[#emph[Tolle de Ecclesia honorabile connubium, nonne reples eam concubinarijs incestuosis, seminifluis mollibus, masculorum concubitoribus, & omni denique genere immundorum? Bernard. in Cant. Serm.] 66. #emph[vide Bernard. de Conuers. ad Cler. cap.] 29.] Fearful have been the effects of this Diabolical doctrine: as fornication, adultery, incest, Sodomy, buggery, and what not? Many wives put from their husbands, because their husbands were Ministers, and many Ministers put from their calling because they had wives: many children by this means basely born, and among them many in their infancy cruelly murdered. #footnote[Huldricus, seu vt alij, Volusianus in Epist. ad Nichol. Pap.] Six thousand heads of infants were found in the ponds of a religious house. How many more thousands have been from time to time cast into other ponds, or buried in gardens, or other places, or other ways conveyed out of sight? Devilish must that doctrine needs be, which hath such devilish effects. #footnote[#emph[Vtinam qui continere non valent perfectionem temerariè profiteri, aut coelebatui dare nomina vererentur. Sumptuosa siquidem turris est, & verbum grande, quod non omnes capere possunt. Bernard. de Conuers. ad Cler. serm.] 29.] Well did he wish, that wished that all they who cannot contain, would take heed how they do rashly profess perfection, and vow virginity.

=== §. 7. #emph[Of the things which are absolutely necessary to make a person fit for marriage.]

They who have power to marry must be careful in choosing #emph[an help meet for them:] for #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 18. Necessary for the essence of marriage.] this was Gods care when first he instituted marriage. To make an help meet for marriage, some things are absolutely necessary for the very essence or being of marriage; others, necessary for the comfort and happiness of marriage.

In regard of the former sort, there must be chosen,

1. #emph[One of the same kind] or nature: for among all the creatures which were made, #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 20. Buggery.] #emph[there was not found an help meet for man:] therefore God out of his bone and flesh made a woman of his #footnote[1\. Persons of the same kind.] own nature and kind.

Contrary to this is the detestable sin of buggery with beasts, expressly forbidden by the #footnote[#emph[Lev.] 18. 23.] law. A sin more then beastly: for the bruit beasts content themselves with their own kind: Monstrous it is in the kind thereof: and a cause of abominable monsters. #footnote[#emph[Vide Aug. de Civit. Dei lib.] 15. #emph[cap.] 23. #emph[& Lud. Vivem in eundem de Incubis, & Succubis.]] Contrary also is that copulation which witches have with devils: then which none more unnatural, none more prodigious and odious.

2. #emph[One of the contrary sex:] the male must choose a female: the female a male. Thus God having made #emph[Adam] a male, #footnote[2\. Persons of divers sexes. #emph[Maritalis copula requirit sexuum aptitudinem. Bern. in Cant. serm.] 66. Unnatural commixtions. #emph[Rom.] 1. 25, 26.] made #emph[Eve] a female, and joined them in marriage. A conjunction of these different sexes is only fit for increase of mankind, and other marriage duties.

Contrary are those unnatural commixtions of parties of the same sex: which the Apostle reckoneth up as judgments inflicted on the heathen, because #emph[they changed the truth of God into a lie, and worshipped, and served the creature more then the Creator.]

3. #emph[One beyond those degrees of consanguinity and affinity which] #footnote[Persons without the degrees of consanguinity.] #emph[are forbidden by the law of God:] these degrees are expressed by #emph[Moses] Levit. 18. 6, 7, etc. And explained in a table of the degrees of consanguinity and affinity within which none may marry, appointed to be hung up in every Church.

Contrary is incest, a sin not only forbidden by Gods #footnote[Incest. #emph[O mulieris scelus incredibile, & praeter hanc vnam in omni vita inauditum. O libidinem effrenatam & indomitam! O audaciam, singularem! Non timuisse vim deorum, hominumque famam? Vicit pudorem libido, timorem audacia, rationem amentia. Cic. pro. A. Cluent.]] word, but so horrible even to the heathen as (to use the Apostles words, 1 Cor. 5. 1.) #emph[it is not so much as named among the Gentiles.] Excellently is that censure verified by the heathen Orators pathetical exclamation against one #emph[Sassia] who married her son in law, in these words: #emph[O incredible wickedness of a woman, not heard of in any age but this! O unbridled and untamed lust! O singular boldness! Not to fear the power of God, and fame of men! etc. Lust, impudency, madness, overcame shame, fear, and reason.]

What may we now think of the dispensations which the Pope giveth for incestuous marriages, allowed unto great Princes even by the #footnote[§. 24. #emph[cap.] 5.] Tridentine council? Doth he not herein show himself to be #emph[that man of sin, who opposeth and exalteth himself above all that is called God?]

4. #emph[One that is free:] nor married, nor betrothed to another: the law of marriage noteth thus much in this clause, #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 5.] #emph[They two shall be one flesh.] And in that #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 22. 22, 23, 24.] the law inflicteth the same punishment #footnote[2 #emph[Thes.] 2. 3, 4. 4. Persons that are free.] upon the person which being betrothed committeth uncleanness, that it doth upon a married person, it is evident that it is unlawful to marry one betrothed to another, as well as one married to another. So firm is a contract, as the law calleth a betrothed maid, #emph[a wife:] and a betrothed maid might not be put away without a bill of divorce.

Contrary is Bigamy, and Polygamy, whereof #footnote[#emph[Treat.] 1. §. 83.] before: unto which head may be referred marriages with such as have been espoused to others before. These are utterly unlawful.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of the lawfulness of other marriages after one of the married couple is dead.]

#strong[1. #emph[Quest.] Are they who have buried their husband or wife so free, as they may marry again?]

#emph[Answ.] Yea, as free as they who were never before married. The law doth not only permit a widow to marry again: but #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 25. 5, 9.] if her husband died before he had any children, it commanded the next kinsman that was living and free to marry her, that he might raise up seed to his brother deceased: which if he refused to do, a penalty of ignominy was inflicted on him: the widow rejected was to #emph[loose his shoe from his foot,] #footnote[#emph[Non concederet hoc Paulus nisi liceret. At parum dico concedit: vult quoque. Volo, inquit, adolescentiores nubere: nec dubium quin viduas dicat. Bern. serm.] 66. #emph[in Cant.]] #emph[and to spit in his face in the presence of the Elders.] The Apostle expressly saith that a woman, when her husband is dead, is at liberty to be married (1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 39.) yea speaking of young widows he further saith, #emph[I will that they marry] (1 #emph[Tim.] 5. 14.) This liberty which the Prophet of God, and Apostle of Christ grant to a wife, can by no show of reason be denied to an husband: for the bond of marriage giveth them a like power over one anothers body, (1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 4.) and knitteth one as inviolably as the other, #emph[(Mat.] 19. 6.) husbands therefore as well as wives have used this liberty, as #emph[Abraham (Gen.] 25. 1.) #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 7. 2.] The Apostle that giveth this liberty, rendereth a reason thereof, taken from the limitation of that time wherein married persons have power one over another, and that is the time of this life only: #emph[For the woman which hath an husband is bound by the law to her husband, so long as he liveth: but if the husband be dead she is loosed, etc.] On this ground all the #footnote[§. 24. #emph[Quid largam benedictionem nuptiarum restringis? Bern. loc. citat. Dominus tot maritorum mulierem nulla suae sententiae significatione damnauit. Aug. de bon. Vid. c.] 12.] reasons which warrant or move such as never were married, to marry, may be applied to them that by death have their yoke-fellow taken from them.

#strong[2. #emph[Quest.] May this liberty be extended any further then to a second marriage?]

#emph[Answ.] We find no restraint from a third, or fourth, or more marriages, if by the divine providence so many wives, or husbands one after another be taken away while there is need for the surviving party to use the benefit of marriage. The woman of Samaria that had five husbands one after another, is not blamed for being married to so many, but for living with one (after the other were dead) that was not her husband, #emph[(Joh.] 4. 18.) Neither did the Lord condemn that woman which was said to have seven husbands one after another, #emph[(Mat.] 22. 25, etc.)

Contrary is the opinion of #footnote[#emph[Secundas nuptias pro fornicationibus habent. Aug. de haer.] §. 26.] Montanists, and Cataphryges, ancient heretics that accounted those marriages which the survivor made after the death of a yoke-fellow, to be adulterous: with which heresy #footnote[#emph[Tertullianus factus est haereticus quia transiens ad Cataphryges cepit secundas nuptias tanquam stupra damnare. Ibid.] §. 86.] #emph[Tertullian] an ancient and learned father was so far infected, as he wrote a treatise in defence thereof. It seemeth by their arguments that one main ground of their error was a misinterpretation of those Scriptures which forbid men to have two wives at once, and women to have two husbands at once, as, #emph[Two shall be one flesh,] Gen. 2. 24. #emph[A Bishop and a Deacon must be the husband of one wife,] 1 Tim. 3. 2, 12. #emph[Let a widow be taken that hath been the wife of one husband,] 1 Tim. 5. 9. These are indeed express texts against such Digamists, and Polygamists as have been described #footnote[#emph[Treat.] 1. §. 83.] before: but they make no more against second or other after-marriages one mate being dead, then against first marriages. Into the roll of these heretics may they be put, who deny such after marriages to any kind of Ministers. So do our adversaries: they #footnote[#emph[Ministerijs minorum ordinum exercendis suffici possunt coniugati, dummodo non bigami. Concil. Trid.] §. 23. #emph[c.] 17. #emph[Vide Bellarm. de Cler. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 23.] exclude such as are married again after one wife is dead from such functions of inferior orders, as they admit those who are but once married unto. They allege many of those Scriptures which Montanists do (as 1 #emph[Tim.] 3. 2, 12. And 5. 9) which showeth that they are infected with the same heresy, though they pretend to renounce it.

=== §. 9. #emph[Of equality in years betwixt husband and wife.]

That matrimonial society may prove comfortable, it is requisite #footnote[#emph[Aequalem ducito Pittaci dictum. Si vis nubere, nube pari. Ouid.] 1. Equality in age.] that there should be some equality betwixt the parties that are married in #emph[Age, estate, Condition, Piety.]

1. For #emph[Age,] as the party that seeketh a mate must be of ripe years, fit to give consent, and able to perform marriage duties, so the mate which is taken must be somewhat answerable #footnote[ἧλιξ ἥλικα. #emph[Arist. Eth. l.] 8. #emph[c.] 12. #emph[Luk.] 1. 7.] in age: if one young, both young: if one of middle age, both so: if one grown to years, the other also. It is noted of #emph[Zachary] and #emph[Elizabeth,] that #emph[both were well stricken in years.] If both were old together, then both also were young together. Equality in years maketh married persons more fit for procreation of children, for a mutual performance of marriage duties each to other, and for making their company and society every way more happy.

This equality is not over strictly to be taken, as if the married couple were to be just of the same age, but only for some answerableness in years: which may be though there be a disparity #footnote[Most meet that husbands be elder then wives.] of five or ten, or somewhat more years: especially if the excess of years be on the husbands part: for besides that according to the ordinary course of nature a mans strength and vigour lasteth longer then a womans, it is very meet that the husband should be somewhat elder then his wife, because he is an head, a governor, a protector of his wife. The Scripture noteth many husbands to be elder then their wives (as #emph[Abraham] was ten years elder then #emph[Sarah;] and if we #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 17. 17.] narrowly mark the circumstances of the histories of #emph[Isaac] and #emph[Jacob,] and their wives, we shall find that the husbands were elder then their wives.) To my remembrance an approved example of an husband younger then his wife cannot be given out of Scripture.

Contrary to this equality in years, is the practise of many #footnote[Unmeet for aged persons to marry young ones.] men and women, who being aged, to satisfy their lust, or for some other by-respect, marry such as are but in the flour of their age, wherein they do many times much fail of their expectation: for those young ones finding the society of aged folks to be burdensome, and irksome unto them, soon begin to loath the same, and thereby cause more grief and vexation, then ever they did give comfort and contentment.

On the other side, others there be who in the prime and #footnote[Unmeet for young persons to marry aged.] strength of their age, for wealth, honour, or such like respects, marry those that with age begin to be decrepit, and unfit to be married, hoping that they will not long live, but that with a little trouble they shall purchase much dignity or riches, & after a while be free again. But God oft meeteth with such in their kind, by prolonging the life of those aged persons, and so making the burden to be much more grievous and tedious then was imagined, and by taking away those young ones sooner then they looked for, whereby it cometh to pass that all their hopes perish. #footnote[#emph[Ne discrepent aetates sic vt vir suscipere liberos possit; mulier non possit; aut haec possit, ille non possit. Ex his enim seditiones, discordiaeque, etc. Arist. Pol. lib.] 7.] The heathen observed inequality in years to be occasions of many mischiefs, and thereupon prescribed rules against it.

=== §. 10. #emph[Of equality in estate and condition betwixt those that are to be married together.]

2. Some equality in outward estate and wealth is also befitting #footnote[Equality in estate. #emph[Premitur magno coniuge nupta minor. Ouid. epist.] 9.] the parties that are to be married together, lest the disparity therein (especially if it be over-great) make the one insult over the other more then is meet: for if a man of great wealth be married to a poor woman, he will think to make her as his maid-servant, and expect that she should carry her self towards him so as beseemeth not a yoke-fellow, and a bedfellow: so as such an one may rather be said to be brought unto bondage, then marriage. And if a rich woman marry a poor man, she will look to be the master, and to rule him: so as the order which God hath established will be clean perverted: and the honour of marriage laid in the dust. For where no order is, there can be no honour.

3. The like may be said of outward condition, that therein #footnote[3\. Equality in condition. #emph[Eas vxores quas inferioris gradus homines ducant, hi qui altioris dignitatis sunt ducere legibus propter dignitatem prohibentur.]] also there be some equality: that Princes, Nobles, and Gentlemen, marry such as are of their own rank: and the meaner sort such as are of their degree. Note what sort of wives #emph[Abraham, Isaac,] and #emph[Jacob] married, and it will appear that they had respect to this parity. Disparity in condition as well as in estate, is a means to make men and women swell and insult above that which is meet: yea and to twit one another in the teeth with their former estate and condition.

Contrary on the one side are the practises of such as affect #footnote[#emph[Digest. lib.] 23. #emph[cap.] 2. §. 49. A fault to affect to marry a person much above ones rank. #emph[Qui longè praestantiores se ducunt, non coniugum viri, sed serui fiunt dotium. Plut. de Instit. liber. Pro.] 14. 1. and 12. 4.] to marry above their own estate and degree: thinking by such marriages to advance themselves. This is the only thing which many seek after in seeking wives and husbands; whereby it cometh to pass that they oft meet with the worst matches, and make their marriage a kind of bondage unto them. Great portions make many women proud, dainty, lavish, idle, and careless; a man were much better, even for help of his outward estate, to marry a prudent, sober, thrifty, careful, diligent wife, though with a small portion, then such an one. A proud back, a dainty tooth, and a lavish hand will soon consume a great portion; but #emph[a wise woman buildeth her house:] and #emph[a virtuous woman is a crown to her husband.] Many wives also that are married to very rich husbands, are more stinted and pinched in their allowance, then such as are married to men of meaner estate. It is not the means which a man hath, but his mind and disposition that maketh him free and bountiful to his wife.

Contrary are the marriages which men of great authority #footnote[A fault to marry a person much under ones rank.] and ability make with mean women, yea their own maids many times, and those of the lowest rank, their kitchen-maids. And which women of noble blood, and great estate, #footnote[#emph[Semper in coniunctionibus non solum quid liceat considerandum est, sed & quid honestum sit. Digest. lib.] 23. #emph[tit.] 2. §. 42.] make with their servingmen. Do they not herein bewray much baseness of mind, and violence of lusts?

If it be said that such marriages are not simply unlawful, the rule of the civil law giveth a good answer, #emph[Always in marriages not only what is lawful, but what is honest and meet, is to be considered.]

=== §. 11. #emph[Of equality in piety and religion betwixt those that are to be married together.]

4. The parity which is of greatest consequence betwixt #footnote[4\. Equality in piety.] parties to be married, is in #emph[piety.] In which respect it is requisite that as a Christian be married to a Christian, and a professor of #footnote[#emph[E domibus Christianis coniugij quaeratur copula. Ambr. Epist.] 70.] the true faith to a professor of the same faith; so one that in truth feareth God, to one of the same mind and disposition, as it is noted of #emph[Zachary] and #emph[Elizabeth, they were both righteous before God, etc.] A worthy couple: one worthy of another: being both alike in such excellent qualities, they could #footnote[#emph[Luk.] 1. 6.] not but reap each from other much comfort, and profit every way.

This is one of the most principal points that are comprised under that proviso given by the holy Ghost in choosing a yoke fellow, in these words, #emph[In the Lord.] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 39.]

There is no such means to increase love, preserve peace, provoke unto all duty, make helpful one to another in all things, and at all times, as this parity. Hereby shall they be made both able to do more good one to another, and capable to receive more good one from another: especially in the best things, even in those which concern their spiritual edification in this world, and eternal salvation in the world to come. For Christ is the fountain and head of all spiritual life and grace, (#emph[it pleased the father that in him should all fulness] #footnote[#emph[Col.] 1. 19.] #emph[dwell: so as it is he that filleth all in all things.]) Now Christ communicateth #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 1. 23.] that life and grace which is in him to those that are members of his body: If then I being a member of that mystical body, be linked by that near and inviolable bond of marriage to one of the members of that body, what hope is there of mutual communicating one to another, and mutual partaking one from another of those gifts and graces which either of us receive from Christ our head? If an unbelieving wife may be saved by a believing husband, and an unbelieving #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 16.] husband by a believing wife: much more will one believer be more and more edified by another.

Happy is that family where both the governors thereof husband and wife are mutual members of Christs body: there will the house be made Gods Church, as the house of #emph[Aquila] and #emph[Priscilla] was. Gods worship will there be maintained. #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 16. 5.] Children will there be trained up in the nurture of the Lord: and servants also taught the fear of God: for they that are indeed of Christs kingdom, will be as leaven which #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 13. 33.] seasoneth the whole lump: instance the profession which #emph[Joshua] #footnote[#emph[Jos.] 24. 15.] maketh to this purpose: and the effect which is noted of the ruler whose son Christ cured, #emph[he himself believed and] #footnote[#emph[Joh.] 4. 53.] #emph[his whole house.]

Here by the way, let me exhort parents & other governors #footnote[Exhortation to marry in the Lord. See #emph[Treat.] 3. §. 68.] of children, both to train up their own children in true piety & fear of God, and also to seek such matches for them, as they may have some assurance that they are of the same faith, and of the same mind and heart: thus shall they procure to their children much happiness in their marriage as #emph[Abraham] did to #emph[Isaac.] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 24.]

If parents ought in this respect to procure the good of their children, much more ought they who are of themselves, and have liberty to marry with whom they will, have an especial respect to parity in piety: especially females, because they are in subjection to their husbands: yet ought not men to be careless herein: for wives, if the fear of God possess not their hearts, though they be the weaker vessels, do oft make their husbands plain vassals to them.

=== §. 12. #emph[Of marriages betwixt persons of divers professions.]

Contrary are marriages with persons of different dispositions, and #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 7. 3. #emph[Neh.] 10. 30.] divers professions in religion: especially when they are made with infidels or idolaters.

This is one of those unequal yokes, wherewith #footnote[2 #emph[Cor.] 6. 14.] the Apostle #footnote[Damages of unequal matches in religion.] forbiddeth Christians to be yoked.

Great are the dangers and damages that ordinarily fall out thereupon. For first the #footnote[#emph[Ezr.] 9. 2.] #emph[holy seed] (to use the words of the holy Ghost) is thereby #emph[mixed:] whereby it cometh to pass that #footnote[#emph[Mal.] 2. 11.] #emph[the holiness of the Lord is profaned,] that is, they whom God hath chosen to be an holy nation, and a peculiar people to him, are polluted.

#emph[Object.] #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 14.] #emph[The unbelieving husband is sanctified by the wife, and the unbelieving wife by the husband.]

#emph[Answ.] That is noted only as a remedy of an inconvenience, which being fallen out cannot be recalled: as when two infidels being married together, the one of them is converted to the true faith and the other remaineth an infidel, that the believer might with some comfort enjoy the company of the #footnote[#emph[Non de ijs qui adhuc coniuncti non sunt, sed de coniunctis agitur. Siquidem non dicit si quis vult accipere infidelem, sed si quis habet infidelem. Chrysost. in] 1 #emph[Cor.] 7. #emph[hom.] 19.] other, the Apostle showeth that for matrimonial communion the unbeliever is sanctified by the believer. But hereby he giveth no liberty to believers to marry with unbelievers. For though God in mercy thus sanctify the unequal yoke which cannot be broken, yet they who professing Gods name do wittingly undergo that yoke, do as much as in them lieth, profane Gods holiness.

2. The professor of the true religion is thereby in danger of Apostasy. Which reason #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 7. 4.] the law it self presseth against this point: and the Scripture giveth many examples of the woeful issue thereof: as of #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 11. 4.] #emph[Solomon] the wisest among men, #footnote[#emph[Judg.] 16. 16.] of #emph[Samson] the strongest, of #footnote[2 #emph[King.] 8. 18.] #emph[Jehoram] and others.

#emph[Object.] If I marry one of a contrary religion, I may convert a sinner from going astray, and save a soul from death.

#emph[Answ.] It is but #emph[a mere may,] thou hast no warrant to make that venture, but art forbid to avoid the contrary danger. There is great cause of fear that in an unequal yoke the worse will prevail over the better. We are naturally prone to yield to error, falsehood, and all manner of evil. Truth, virtue and grace, are against the current of nature. It is like to fall out in this unequal yoke, as if two oxen should in one yoke draw contrary ways one against the other on the side of an hill; that ox which draweth down-hill, though it be a poor, lean, weak one, will draw down the other, though it should be an able, lusty, strong one. All evil is down hill: all goodness is up hill: and by reason of the flesh which remaineth in all, so long as they remain on earth, they who have some goodness in them are prone and ready to fall away: but they which are evil are very obstinate therein. Yea evil also is of a more spreading and infecting nature. A little sour leaven will soon make a great batch of dough sour: but a great deal of sweet dough will not sweeten a little leaven. It is therefore an unwarrantable pretext for any to marry one of a contrary religion, or disposition, on hope to convert them. It is not in mans power to convert another. It is a divine work, without Gods blessing it cannot be done. Little ground there is to expect a blessing from God in that course which is directly against his own ordinance. A curse may rather be feared. And woeful experience of all ages hath showed how God hath cursed such marriages.

#emph[Object.] Saint #footnote[1 #emph[Pet.] 3. 1.] #emph[Peter] exhorteth wives so to carry themselves, as their husbands, #emph[which obey not the word,] may by that means #emph[be won without the word,] and #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 16.] Saint #emph[Paul] saith to such as were unequally matched, #emph[what knowest thou, ô wife, whether thou shalt save thy husband? Or how knowest thou, ô man, whether thou shalt save thy wife?] It appeareth therefore that an evil husband may be converted by a good wife: and an evil wife by a good husband.

#emph[Ans.] These directions are noted only as a remedy to redress such irrevocable mischiefs as are fallen out, and not to give liberty of rushing wittingly into them (as was before noted.) When an ill event is fallen out, necessity lieth on a man; so as all means is to be used to redress the same. But thereupon to infer that a man may safely enter into that course which draweth a man to those mischiefs, what is it but to thrust himself into the fire, and to cast himself into temptation, #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 6. 13.] which he is taught to pray against? Besides, the forenamed directions are not grounded on any express promises, but upon mere suppositions.

3. Many other mischiefs are like to follow upon this kind of unequal marriage, besides danger of apostasy (which yet is the greatest) as

1. #footnote[#emph[Nihil grauius quam copulari alienigenae, vbi discordiae incentiua conflantur. Ambr. Ep.] 70.] #emph[Many jars and contentions betwixt man and wife.] For how can there be mutual marriage-concord, betwixt those that are of different minds?

2. #emph[Disturbance of the family:] one part holding with the man, another with the woman.

3. #emph[Wasting of goods:] the husband spending what he can on such as are of his profession: and the wife what she can, on them that are of hers.

4. #emph[Hindrance of all holy duties] in the house: the party that is of #footnote[#emph[Cum oratio communis esse debeat, quomodo inter dispares deuotione potest esse coniugij communis charitas? Ibid.]] the contrary profession not enduring that there should be any.

5. #emph[Profaneness and Atheism in children.] For commonly when parents are of divers religions, children are of none: #footnote[#emph[Lev.] 24. 10, 11.] instance that cursed blasphemer, whose mother was an Israelite, but his father an Egyptian.

The Scripture noteth such children to be #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 6. 4.] monsters, and #footnote[#emph[Neh.] 13. 24.] mongrels.

#emph[Object. Salmon] married #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 1. 5.] #emph[Rahab,] and #emph[Boaz] married #emph[Ruth,] both which women were infidels.

#emph[Answ.] They had been so, but were not so when they married them. Faith and repentance, as they alter the disposition, so also the condition of the worst that can be. #footnote[#emph[Jos.] 2. 9, etc. #emph[Heb.] 11. 31.] #emph[Rahabs] faith was manifested before she was married to #emph[Salmon:] and #footnote[#emph[Ruth] 1. 16.] #emph[Ruth] had forsaken her country gods, and chosen the God of Israel to be her God: and #footnote[#emph[Ruth] 2. 11, 12.] #emph[Boaz] knew as much.

#emph[Object.] But no such thing can be said of the #footnote[#emph[Judg.] 14. 1, etc.] daughter of the Philistines whom #emph[Samson] married.

#emph[Answ.] This was an extraordinary matter, and therefore not exemplary for all. That he did it by an extraordinary instinct is evident by this phrase, #emph[it was of the Lord:] that it is not exemplary is evident, because #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 7. 3.] Gods law, the rule of example, forbids it. God moved him thereto to manifest his own #emph[power] and #emph[wisdom] thereby.

His #emph[power,] in that he was able to work by contraries: as to work enmity by amity; utter destruction by near conjunction: for God turned the heart of #emph[Samson,] a chief man in Israel, to affect, and desire in marriage, a daughter of the Philistines: and moved the parents of that maid to give her to #emph[Samson] in marriage: here was amity and near conjunction. Yet #footnote[#emph[Judg.] 15.] the history showeth that by this means an occasion was given to #emph[Samson] to revenge himself, and the people of God on the Philistines.

His #emph[Wisdom,] in that he turned that which had an apparent show of great evil, into much good. For, that #emph[Samson] a Nazarite of the Lord, a chief man in Israel, should join in marriage with a daughter of idolaters, who also were spiteful enemies of the Lords people, carried a show of evil to such as knew not the spirit of #emph[Samson,] nor the intent of the Lord: yet God by this means brought #emph[Samson] to be a Judge and deliverer of his people.

By such a spirit as #emph[Elijah] called for fire from heaven, did #footnote[2 #emph[King.] 1. 10.] #emph[Samson] marry an idolater. If any hence shall think of marrying Idolaters, I may say to them as Christ to his disciples, #emph[ye know not of what spirit ye are.] This example of #emph[Samson] can #footnote[#emph[Luk.] 9. 55.] be no more warrant to others to marry with idolaters, then his practise in pulling down an house on his head, to self-murderers. #footnote[#emph[Judg.] 16. 30.] The warrant of extraordinary matters is particular, proper and peculiar to them only that do them. But the warrant of exemplary matters must be the general and known will of God in his word.

Whether in some particular cases this general rule may be dispensed withal or not, I leave to the determination of more learned Divines, and submit all to the judgment of Gods Church.

=== §. 13. #emph[Of that mutual liking which must pass betwixt marriageable persons before they be married.]

Having showed what #emph[persons] are fit to be joined in marriage, it remaineth, to show after what #emph[manner] they are to be joined.

There are in Scripture three steps or degrees commended unto us by which marriageable parties are in order to proceed unto marriage.

1. A mutual liking.

2. An actual contract.

3. A public solemnization of marriage.

I. The first liking is sometimes on the parents or other friends part, and then by them made known to the party to be married, as the friends of #emph[Rebekah,] liking the offer of #emph[Isaac] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 24. 58.] which was made by #emph[Abrahams] servant, made it known to #emph[Rebekah] her self. Sometimes again the first liking is on the parties part that is to be married: and then if that party be under the government of parents, the matter must be moved to them, before there be any further proceeding therein, as #emph[Samson] who seeing and liking a daughter of the Philistines, #footnote[#emph[Judg.] 14. 2.] told his father and his mother thereof. Yea though the party be not under the government of any, yet it is very meet that counsel be taken of wise and understanding friends: that in a matter so weighty as marriage is, there may be the advice of more heads then one, for the preventing of such mischiefs as through rashness might fall out. After a liking is thus taken by one party of a meet mate, that liking must be moved to the other party so liked, to know whether there be a reciprocal affection of one towards another. Thus #emph[Samson] went and #footnote[#emph[Judg.] 14. 7.] talked with that woman whom he liked to be his wife. If at first there be a good liking mutually and thoroughly settled in both their hearts of one another, love is like to continue in them for ever, as things which are well glued, and settled before they be shaken up and down, will never be severed asunder: but if they be joined together without glue, or shaken while the glue is moist, they cannot remain firm.

Mutual love and good liking of each other is as glue.

Let the parties to be married be herein well settled before they come to meet with trials through cohabitation, and that love will not easily be loosened by any trials.

Contrary is the adulterous and brutish practise of such as #footnote[Too eager and hasty pursuing after marriage, a brutish thing.] so soon as they cast their eye on any whom they like, never advise or consult about a right and due proceeding unto marriage, but instantly with all the eagerness and speed they can, like brute beasts, seek to have their desire and lust satisfied. Though to keep themselves free from the penalty of the laws under which they live, they procure means to be married, yet they declare a lustful and adulterous mind. And their practise is too like to the practise of the Benjamites, who catched wives from among the daughters of #emph[Shiloh] as they were dancing: or else to the practise of the old world, which so grieved the Spirit of God, that it repented him that he had #footnote[#emph[Judg.] 21. 23.] made man, and thereupon he was moved to bring a general deluge on the whole world. Their practise was this, that #emph[they] #emph[took them wives of all that they chose:] that is, they rashly and #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 6. 2.] suddenly married whomsoever they liked, without any consideration of their condition.
